On Aug 5, 2010, at 8:04 AM, David Hunter <dhunter@stat.psu.edu<mailto:dhunter@stat.psu.edu>> wrote:

Hi Jenine.  The base argument does in fact work with nodemix.  This is a bit clunky, but it's not hard to do.  Take a look at this example:

 data(faux.mesa.high)
 summary(faux.mesa.high~nodemix("Race"))
mix.Race.Black.Black  mix.Race.Black.Hisp   mix.Race.Hisp.Hisp mix.Race.Black.NatAm
                   0                    8                   53                   13
 mix.Race.Hisp.NatAm mix.Race.NatAm.NatAm mix.Race.Black.Other  mix.Race.Hisp.Other
                  41                   46                    0                    1
mix.Race.NatAm.Other mix.Race.Other.Other mix.Race.Black.White  mix.Race.Hisp.White
                   0                    0                    5                   22
mix.Race.NatAm.White mix.Race.Other.White mix.Race.White.White
                  10                    0                    4

Suppose I wanted to omit all pairings involving the "Other" category.  Notice that in the summary above, there are 15 total positions in the vector and "Other" occurs in positions 7, 8, 9, 10, and 14.  Therefore, I can make these positions the base and get rid of them:

 summary(faux.mesa.high~nodemix("Race", base=c(7,8,9,10,14)))
mix.Race.Black.Black  mix.Race.Black.Hisp   mix.Race.Hisp.Hisp mix.Race.Black.NatAm
                   0                    8                   53                   13
 mix.Race.Hisp.NatAm mix.Race.NatAm.NatAm mix.Race.Black.White  mix.Race.Hisp.White
                  41                   46                    5                   22
mix.Race.NatAm.White mix.Race.White.White
                  10                    4


Hope this helps.
Dave



